  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by the equation $s(t)=2{t}^{4}-3t+5$, where $t\\ge 0$ is measured in seconds. At how many seconds is the particle's velocity equal to $509\\,\\text{m/s}?$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-3t+5) \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to know when the velocity is $509\\,\\text{m/s}$, we substitute $v(t)=509$ into the equation above and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}509 & =8{t}^{3}-3 \\\\ 512 & =8{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & =\\frac{512}{8} \\\\ {t}^{3} & =64 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle is $509\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$",
